WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Glen Reynolds, Founder of Circus Road Films:&nbsp;
This has not been exposed anywhere in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.800
no festival or anything. I'm giving you&nbsp;
and a few other people a look at it first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.240
Sometimes that's a better message to a distributor

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.720
Film Courage: Glen, how do&nbsp;
you get paid? Please tell us.&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:20.480
Glen: A couple of different&nbsp;
ways. So always a commission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:28.320
a percentage of whatever my filmmaker makes.&nbsp;
So there's an advance from the distributor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:35.200
or royalties paid later on. I usually get a&nbsp;
commission on whatever and that's a negotiable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.880
commission depending on the circumstances of the&nbsp;
film, whether it's already in a major festival or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.800
if I'm working on it from the beginning,&nbsp;
we can negotiate what the commission is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.560
Often I also work for a consulting fee as&nbsp;
well because I spend most producer reps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:00.720
I would say tend to throw films against the wall&nbsp;
to see if they stick. If they don't make the deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:09.520
move on. I deal with very much true independent&nbsp;
films that need a lot of hand-holding and a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.600
tender loving care and so I fashion myself both as&nbsp;
a producer rep and a consultant. I'm usually paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.000
an upfront fee in addition to my commission really&nbsp;
to help with the fact that I'm going to negotiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
the contract. I'm going to handle all that. I'm&nbsp;
going to spend time with them to make sure they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.520
understand the industry, understand the buyers,&nbsp;
help out maybe getting them an international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:39.840
sales agent. I try to fashion myself as kind of&nbsp;
a distribution consultant and a producer rep.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.720
Film Courage: Are all your fees like one-time&nbsp;
fees or are there any that are continual?

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:44.960
Glen: Just one time.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:45.680
Film Courage: Just one time?

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:52.640
Glen: Yes, one time. Usually an upfront fee and&nbsp;
then in the way I do it I don't I don't put a term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:01.200
on my deals. I feel like I'm hired for however&nbsp;
long it takes me to get the film distribution or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.840
a festival (I’m also brought on as a festival&nbsp;
advocate). Quite often that's part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.560
the fee structure is that I'm going to advocate&nbsp;
the film to festivals and programmers I've gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.760
to know over time to try and help it get some&nbsp;
kind of promotional boost before we sell the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.160
so I've gotten to know a lot of programmers over&nbsp;
the years so sometimes I'm brought in pre-festival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:27.120
to call programmers and just make sure they're&nbsp;
watching the movie. Also to pitch it but I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.720
the biggest thing there is just to make sure&nbsp;
they're watching it. If you add up the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.040
entries into these festivals and you look at the&nbsp;
number of people that are been hired to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.000
them it's almost impossible right? That this&nbsp;
few number of people are watching this number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.560
of movies over a three-month period so their films&nbsp;
sometimes are not getting watched or they're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.160
being watched by an intern or an underling.&nbsp;
So I'm there to try to make sure that someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.000
of significance is watching&nbsp;
the movie and given a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.720
in addition to maybe consulting my filmmakers&nbsp;
on what festivals to apply to. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:10.000
right for Sundance? Is it a SXSW kind of&nbsp;
movie? If not, is it more of a Heartland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.840
Seattle there's all kinds of great festivals&nbsp;
but there are different festivals of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.840
strategies. I'm here for that kind&nbsp;
of in addition to everything else.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.960
